#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "803b5b7d-19a4-5591-93e1-89822232d2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126459} {:answer/id #uuid "2b499760-85ab-586c-99be-e4f9733e71a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126460} {:answer/id #uuid "3546a93e-b198-5bd4-b10c-23f90617dede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126461} {:answer/id #uuid "5517953e-15ef-5bac-8909-234f6722fac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126462} {:answer/id #uuid "7952fe66-007f-5434-a288-817200c71d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5517953e-15ef-5bac-8909-234f6722fac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126462} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f8a301-f3e5-5380-913d-63c617aab3d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba3c81ec-9070-50b3-af3a-82e4e50bb35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160380" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)}=6(1)=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78e50f2-df92-5d32-b021-3678fecb2653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352996} {:answer/id #uuid "82b14d9a-5b24-5452-bb9e-25b311f1c6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352997} {:answer/id #uuid "57f39cb0-b84e-55cd-83ee-42d45899064c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352998} {:answer/id #uuid "a6588f46-d395-578b-acd2-0c57d12e187e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352999} {:answer/id #uuid "674dad15-21e8-5183-a4b7-782ee5898f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f39cb0-b84e-55cd-83ee-42d45899064c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352998} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a70ac85-cca7-5847-8ac7-2f1243b716d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "358311d9-2c61-59cc-ac39-8891f3d6b2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-160427" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8764afe1-5764-567f-865b-1c9c8ecf56f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353003} {:answer/id #uuid "ef68a1cd-c50f-5e7f-a93a-6d1a9b4982e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353004} {:answer/id #uuid "6684e08a-371e-5fff-97eb-28b1ffeb02d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353005} {:answer/id #uuid "dc78e82c-5c0c-56aa-b67c-b9af539233a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/c501e7e341af57e86f18d73871314800d453f79572e16ccf2477335352d4dcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353006} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd300db-ff65-5c8f-8c62-78197bbaf410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/9cd254ac65dedfa4c720b18afd4f5c3c7676b4f21170930f78e9adac276317ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc78e82c-5c0c-56aa-b67c-b9af539233a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/c501e7e341af57e86f18d73871314800d453f79572e16ccf2477335352d4dcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353006} :answer-field/id #uuid "f28f83d4-1902-5103-870c-3bf0ae775300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a1b487f-874e-5662-8cb4-0ff2c90e3298" :question/math-academy-id "q-161843" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4u+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4u+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2v \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =4+4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+4uv & >0 \\\\ 4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>-\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<-\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/c501e7e341af57e86f18d73871314800d453f79572e16ccf2477335352d4dcdc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b38b5a8-fcd3-5729-95f3-d2b67868d894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/4d944eb13437b86c0cf6ae4fa8bca09031ed0f2f85a9f20bcb0050a6de9f8ebb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353010} {:answer/id #uuid "e2710562-98ac-5e9b-bb7e-966ec4f09f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353011} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1def52-dd46-56d3-9c0f-d25733d66390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353012} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7311c2-b4c7-5f25-887d-9d8a7656ca4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353013} {:answer/id #uuid "4b30bfe3-68ef-50a0-86ca-f51df375ec2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/f7bc22ac4dfd2d7015952144ece63f91ecccef2a992649aece60dc8a70f1d018.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd1def52-dd46-56d3-9c0f-d25733d66390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353012} :answer-field/id #uuid "1241b385-e434-5aec-b1e8-a0c74101ae4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e552dcc6-87b8-5585-84f4-2c455a9099f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161856" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cc7022d-903a-524c-a8ea-addf0b82c712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353017} {:answer/id #uuid "e5908624-e088-5502-9da6-fc83b968a98b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353018} {:answer/id #uuid "c684e0eb-7993-5acb-9c53-331e6b7f10a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353019} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fc70f0-c0c1-51aa-a411-c59cfb97a1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353020} {:answer/id #uuid "d894cb69-b0c6-5a21-88c8-bbbb12846405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d894cb69-b0c6-5a21-88c8-bbbb12846405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353021} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92581a9-0527-5110-b05f-748971ebd883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c4b0cb3-e283-5cdd-b9d4-c83ef946d645" :question/math-academy-id "q-161873" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}})\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
